1
00:00:01,939 --> 00:00:16,159
This is PACT, a Nimiq Pay mini app that turns a real work
agreement into a traceable, verified payment. A brand posts
a clear brief, both sides sign the exact terms with their
wallets, and payment is checked on chain.

2
00:00:16,665 --> 00:00:33,080
The landing page explains who it's for, brands and
contributors, and the four steps: brief, agree, deliver, and
pay. It also says plainly what PACT is not. It never holds
funds. Money goes straight from wallet to wallet.

3
00:00:33,586 --> 00:00:47,941
Inside Nimiq Pay you would continue with your wallet. In a
normal browser we use the demo, which is clearly marked as
sample data. The first time you sign in, PACT asks how you
will use it. We'll start as a contributor.

4
00:00:48,447 --> 00:01:00,628
The contributor dashboard is built from real records. At the
top is an invitation a brand sent directly to this account.
Below it are open campaigns and the agreements already in
progress.

5
00:01:01,131 --> 00:01:12,906
Find work lists public campaigns. You can filter by type of
work, by currency, and by whether spots are still open.
Invite only campaigns never appear in this list.

6
00:01:19,065 --> 00:01:30,001
Let's search for a banner job and open it. Each campaign
shows the reward per person, the deadline, how many spots
are left, and the full brief with its requirements.

7
00:01:30,504 --> 00:01:41,157
Before committing, the contributor reads the exact agreement
they would get. The platform, how to submit, and the number
of revision rounds all become signed terms.

8
00:01:41,660 --> 00:01:52,349
Now the invitation. It is bound to this wallet. Nobody else
can see it, open it, or accept it, and a private campaign
never shows up in the public list.

9
00:01:52,854 --> 00:02:02,488
Accepting does not sign anything. It creates the agreement,
and both sides still sign the exact terms with their wallets
before any work starts.

10
00:02:03,592 --> 00:02:15,189
The agreement page shows real progress from saved data:
terms written, both signatures, work submitted, review,
payment sent, and finally verified on the network.

11
00:02:15,696 --> 00:02:22,881
The same wallet can switch roles from the account menu.
Agreements already made stay exactly as they are.

12
00:02:28,880 --> 00:02:43,141
As a brand, the dashboard starts with posting work. Once
campaigns are live, it separates what posting could commit,
what has been agreed, and what has been verified as paid. A
budget is never money set aside.

13
00:02:43,644 --> 00:02:54,999
Posting work takes a title, a brief, the type of work,
requirements, a reward, the number of spots, and a deadline.
This one will be invite only.

14
00:03:04,352 --> 00:03:15,341
Contributors can be invited by their Pact handle or an exact
wallet address. The search shows the wallet next to each
handle, because the invitation is bound to that wallet.

15
00:03:16,375 --> 00:03:28,279
Once it's sent, the brand tracks the invitation and the
people working. The budget shows the most this campaign
could commit, what's been agreed, and only the payments the
network has confirmed.

16
00:03:28,784 --> 00:03:42,444
A wallet sending money only marks a payment as sent. PACT
reads the Nimiq network itself and shows verified only when
the transaction matches the agreement. This demo payment is
sample data, and it says so.

17
00:03:42,954 --> 00:03:48,037
There's a light theme too. By default it simply follows your
phone.

18
00:03:52,019 --> 00:04:01,778
PACT. A clear brief, signed terms, and a trackable payment,
inside Nimiq Pay. It's open source under the MIT license.

